<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nigerian Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1967">1967</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Biafran War, Nigeria–Biafra War, Biafra War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nigerian Civil War was fought from 6 July 1967 to 15 January 1970 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biafra">Biafra</a>, a secessionist state that had declared its independence. Its immediate causes were a military coup, a counter-coup and anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> pogroms in the Northern Region in 1966. Nigerian forces under General Yakubu Gowon surrounded Biafra, led by Lieutenant Colonel Chukwuemeka Odumegwu Ojukwu, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> led to the mass starvation of its civilians: there were approximately 100,000 military casualties, while between 500,000 and 2 million Biafran civilians died of starvation. The Biafran airlift inspired the formation of Doctors Without Borders.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the mid-1970s Nigeria had lived through a decade of military rule after a series of coups and the <b>Nigerian Civil War</b>, and under General Olusegun Obasanjo soldiers were a constant presence in public life.</blockquote><a href="/songs/zombie">Zombie</a></li></ul></section>
